('2527', '7', '2026-03-25 11:04:47', '2026-03-25 18:04:47', '
<p><strong>What are the most important eLearning design principles?</strong></p>



<p>The most important eLearning design principles are rooted in cognitive psychology and user experience, aiming to reduce cognitive load and increase engagement. Key principles include the Multimedia Principle (using graphics to explain, not decorate), the Coherence Principle (removing extraneous &#8220;nice-to-know&#8221; material), the Personalization Principle (writing conversationally), and Segmenting (breaking complex topics into micro-lessons). By applying these rules, instructional designers can move past &#8220;click-next&#8221; fatigue to build scenario-based, interactive courses that genuinely change learner behavior.</p>



<p>We have all endured it. The &#8220;Click Next&#8221; fatigue. The wall of text is narrated by a robotic voice. The quiz that insults your intelligence. Bad eLearning isn&#8217;t just annoying; it is expensive. It wastes employee time, fails to change behavior, and ultimately gives the L&amp;D department a bad reputation.</p>



<p>Great eLearning doesn&#8217;t happen by accident. It happens by following specific eLearning design principles grounded in cognitive psychology and user experience (UX) design. You don&#8217;t need to be a graphic artist to build effective training, but you do need to follow the rules of how the human brain processes information. Here are the 8 essential rules for designing courses that respect your learners and drive actual results. To see these principles in action, review our<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?q=https://www.ninjatropic.com/services/\"> custom eLearning services</a>.</p>



<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full\"><img loading=\"lazy\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"980\" height=\"436\" src=\"https://www.ninjatropic.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/elearning-design-principles.png\" alt=\"- Microlearning\" class=\"wp-image-7220\" title=\"| Corporate training\" srcset=\"https://www.ninjatropic.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/elearning-design-principles.png 980w, https://www.ninjatropic.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/elearning-design-principles-480x214.png 480w\" sizes=\"auto, (min-width: 0px) and (max-width: 480px) 480px, (min-width: 481px) 980px, 100vw\" /></figure>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>1. The Multimedia Principle: Graphics + Text &gt; Text Alone</strong></h2>



<p>This is the golden rule of instructional design. People learn better from words and pictures than from words alone. However, this does not mean you should add decorative clip art just to fill space.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Use visuals to explain, not just decorate.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Use diagrams to show relationships, charts to show data, or screenshots to show software steps.</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Add a stock photo of &#8220;people shaking hands&#8221; just because the slide looks empty. If the visual doesn&#8217;t add instructional value, remove it.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>2. The Contiguity Principle: Keep Related Items Together</strong></h2>



<p>Cognitive load increases when a learner has to scan back and forth to connect information. If you have a diagram on the left and the labels for that diagram in a paragraph on the right, you are forcing the brain to work too hard.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Place text labels physically close to the graphics they describe.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Place the definition of a button right next to the button in the screenshot.</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Put a legend at the bottom of the screen (A, B, C) that forces the user to look up and down repeatedly to understand the image.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>3. The Coherence Principle: Less Is More</strong></h2>



<p>Subject Matter Experts (SMEs) often want to include everything. They will ask you to add &#8220;just one more slide&#8221; on the history of the department or a &#8220;nice-to-know&#8221; fact about the software code.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Remove all extraneous material.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Be ruthless with your editing. If a piece of audio, video, or text does not directly support the learning objective, delete it.</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Add background music. It might seem &#8220;cinematic,&#8221; but for learning, it competes with the narration for the learner&#8217;s attention span. We know how to distill complex topics effectively; see our<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?q=https://www.ninjatropic.com/portfolio/\"> video production portfolio</a>.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>4. The Personalization Principle: Be Human</strong></h2>



<p>Corporate training often falls into the trap of using overly formal, bureaucratic language. Phrases like &#8220;The user should utilize the interface to facilitate transactions&#8221; are dry and distancing.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Write like you speak.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Use &#8220;you&#8221; and &#8220;we.&#8221; Use conversational language. It signals to the learner&#8217;s brain that this is a social exchange, which increases engagement.</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Use passive voice or complex jargon. Instead of &#8220;Compliance must be adhered to,&#8221; say &#8220;You need to follow these rules to stay safe.&#8221;</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>5. Segmenting: Break It Down</strong></h2>



<p>This aligns closely with the shift toward microlearning. The human brain is not a sponge; it is a sieve. If you pour too much information in at once, most of it spills over.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Break complex lessons into user-paced segments.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Allow the learner to control the navigation. Let them click &#8220;Next&#8221; when they are ready, rather than auto-advancing the slides.</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Lock the navigation on a 10-minute video without chapter markers. Learners need the ability to pause, digest, and revisit concepts.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>6. Visual Hierarchy: Guide the Eye</strong></h2>



<p>When a learner opens a slide, their eye instantly searches for where to look first. If everything is bold, nothing is bold. If every color is bright, nothing stands out.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Design a clear path for the eye to follow.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Use size and position to indicate importance. The headline should be the largest text. The key takeaway should be highlighted or in a distinct color box.</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Clutter the screen with 15 competing elements. Use whitespace liberally to separate different ideas.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>7. Meaningful Interactions: No &#8220;Clicky-Clicky&#8221; Bling</strong></h2>



<p>Interactivity is vital, but clicking for the sake of clicking is not learning. Drag-and-drop interactions or &#8220;reveal&#8221; cards are only useful if they challenge the learner to think.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Interactions must mirror real-world decisions.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Build scenario-based questions. Instead of asking &#8220;What does the policy say?&#8221;, ask &#8220;John is facing this situation; what should he do?&#8221;</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Force the learner to click 5 tabs just to read 5 paragraphs of text. That is just reading with extra steps.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>8. Feedback Loops: Explain the &#8220;Why&#8221;</strong></h2>



<p>In many courses, getting a question wrong results in a &#8220;Incorrect, try again&#8221; popup. This is a missed learning opportunity.</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li><strong>The Rule:</strong> Treat the feedback screen as a teaching moment.</li>



<li><strong>Do:</strong> Explain why the answer was wrong. &#8220;That is incorrect because option B would violate safety protocol X. The correct action is C because&#8230;&#8221;</li>



<li><strong>Don&#8217;t:</strong> Just show a score. If a learner guesses correctly but doesn&#8217;t know why, they haven&#8217;t learned anything. Need proof that our strategies work? Check out our<a href=\"https://www.ninjatropic.com/case-studies/\"> association case studies</a>.</li>
</ul>



<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full\"><img loading=\"lazy\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"980\" height=\"437\" src=\"https://www.ninjatropic.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/elearning-design-2.png\" alt=\"- Microlearning\" class=\"wp-image-7221\" title=\"| Corporate training\" srcset=\"https://www.ninjatropic.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/elearning-design-2.png 980w, https://www.ninjatropic.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/elearning-design-2-480x214.png 480w\" sizes=\"auto, (min-width: 0px) and (max-width: 480px) 480px, (min-width: 481px) 980px, 100vw\" /></figure>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Next Steps: Audit Your Current Courses</strong></h2>



<p>You don&#8217;t need to rebuild your entire LMS overnight. Start by applying these eLearning design principles to your next project. Try this simple audit:</p>



<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">
<li>Open your most recent course.</li>



<li>Mute the audio. Can you still understand the key message just from the visuals? (Multimedia Principle)</li>



<li>Look at the text. Are you using &#8220;you&#8221; and &#8220;we&#8221;? (Personalization Principle)</li>



<li>Check the quizzes. Do the wrong answers explain why they are wrong? (Feedback Loops)</li>
</ul>



<p>By strictly adhering to these rules, you will move from creating &#8220;required training&#8221; to creating learning experiences that actually work. Need help applying these principles to your content? We specialize in instructional design that respects the learner. Contact us today via our<a href=\"https://www.ninjatropic.com/contact-us/\"> Contact Page</a> for a design audit.</p>



<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>



<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</strong></h2>



<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Why should I avoid auto-advancing slides in eLearning?</strong></h3>



<p>Auto-advancing slides violate the Segmenting Principle, which dictates that learning should be user-paced. Because the human brain can only process a limited amount of information at once, learners need the ability to control navigation so they can pause, digest, and revisit concepts at their own speed.</p>



<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>&nbsp;What is the Coherence Principle in eLearning design?</strong></h3>



<p>The Coherence Principle is the rule of &#8220;less is more.&#8221; It requires instructional designers to ruthlessly edit out all extraneous, &#8220;nice-to-know&#8221; material—such as background music, decorative graphics, or tangent facts—that do not directly support the primary learning objective.</p>



<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>How do I create meaningful interactions instead of just &#8220;clicking&#8221;?</strong></h3>



<p>To create meaningful interactions, avoid forcing learners to click tabs simply to reveal text. Instead, build scenario-based questions that mirror real-world decisions. For example, instead of asking &#8220;What is the policy limit?&#8221;, ask &#8220;A client requests a refund over the limit. What should you do next?&#8221;.</p>
', 'eLearning Design Principles: 8 Rules for Creating Courses That Actually Work', 'Learn how to apply eLearning design principles to create effective courses. Follow 8 proven rules to improve engagement and learning outcomes.', 'publish', 'closed', 'closed', '', 'elearning-design-principles', '', '', '2026-06-25 20:25:39', '2026-06-25 20:25:39', '', '0', 'http://localhost:8080/elearning-design-principles/', '0', 'post', '', '0'),
('2528', '0', '2026-06-05 22:44:11', '2026-06-05 22:44:11', 'eLearning Design Principles: 8 Rules for Creating Courses That Actually Work. From: eLearning Design Principles: 8 Rules for Creating Courses That Actually Work.', 'eLearning Design Principles: 8 Rules for Creating Courses That Actually Work', 'eLearning Design Principles: 8 Rules for Creating Courses That Actually Work', 'inherit', 'open', 'closed', '', 'elearning-design-principles-8-rules-for-creating-courses-that-actually-work', '', '', '2026-06-05 22:44:11', '2026-06-05 22:44:11', '', '2527', 'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/849123588-illust_4.webp', '0', 'attachment', 'image/webp', '0'),
('2529', '7', '2025-07-13 14:19:20', '2025-07-13 21:19:20', '<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Non-Dues Revenue Training Can Future-Proof Your Association</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let&#8217;s be honest&#8212;most associations didn&#8217;t launch to become business ventures. They were born from passion, purpose, and a shared vision to serve members and advance a cause.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>But even the most purpose-driven mission needs funding.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For years, many associations have leaned on one main pillar: membership dues. But here&#8217;s the catch&#8212;relying solely on dues in today&#8217;s economic landscape is like sailing a ship with one oar. Unpredictable membership numbers, rising operational costs, and the ever-changing digital economy all demand something more sustainable.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>That&#8217;s where non-dues revenue steps in&#8212;and the difference between surviving and thriving often comes down to one thing: training your team to understand and unlock it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This isn&#8217;t about selling out. It&#8217;s about buying into new possibilities&#8212;diversifying revenue while strengthening your mission.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let&#8217;s walk through how non-dues revenue training works, why it matters, and how it can reshape your association&#8217;s future.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Exactly Is Non-Dues Revenue (And Why Does It Matter So Much)?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you&#8217;re still thinking of non-dues revenue as &#8220;nice to have,&#8221; it&#8217;s time to shift your mindset.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Think of non-dues revenue as your mission&#8217;s safety net&#8212;and launchpad.</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It&#8217;s the revenue your association earns outside of membership dues: sponsorships, digital courses, certifications, advertising, events, publications, merchandise, and more.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Here&#8217;s why it&#8217;s critical:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">It creates financial stability.</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Member dues fluctuate. Non-dues revenue builds resilience.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">It empowers innovation.</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You can test new offerings or tech without draining your core budget.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">It enhances member value.</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>With added funding, you can offer more perks without raising dues.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">It fuels your mission.</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>More revenue means more programs, outreach, and long-term impact.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>And here&#8217;s the truth: every department in your association plays a role in making it work, not just leadership.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">A Realistic Look: What Non-Dues Revenue Training <em>Can</em> Unlock</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Picture this: a mid-sized association for HR professionals is at a crossroads. Membership growth has stalled. Event attendance is inconsistent. Their flagship annual conference&#8212;typically a cornerstone of their revenue&#8212;was canceled unexpectedly, leaving a major budget gap.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Sound familiar?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>While this is a composite scenario drawn from challenges we&#8217;ve seen across the industry, it reflects a common reality for many associations today.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In response, this hypothetical association didn&#8217;t overhaul everything overnight. Instead, they started small: a few internal brainstorming sessions, some cross-departmental workshops, and expert guidance from a marketing consultant. The goal? Equip their team to think differently about non-dues revenue.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Here&#8217;s what an association <em>could</em> realistically achieve with the right training and collaboration:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><li>Their education team launches a premium certification program, bringing in over $75,000 in new revenue.</li><li>Marketing collaborates with sponsors to host a branded webinar series, earning $40,000 in partnerships.</li><li>Member engagement rolls out a small but effective merch store, bringing in $12,000 and growing steadily.</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>These outcomes didn&#8217;t require a huge investment&#8212;just a shift in mindset, alignment across departments, and targeted training.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who Needs Non-Dues Revenue Training? (Spoiler: Everyone)</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let&#8217;s bust a myth: Non-dues revenue is not just a job for your development or sales team.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Your events manager can create new value through sponsor packages.<br>Your membership coordinator might spot upsell opportunities.<br>Your education staff can turn member questions into marketable content.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Training gives your team the confidence and context to spot these opportunities&#8212;and take action.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Should Non-Dues Revenue Training Include?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A strong training program equips your team with strategy, skills, and support. Here are the essential building blocks:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Identifying Revenue Opportunities That Align With Your Mission</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You don&#8217;t need to chase every shiny new tactic. Focus on what fits your audience and values. Training sessions should walk your team through:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><li><strong>Sponsorships &amp; Advertising</strong> &#8211; Partnering with mission-aligned brands to bring added value to your members.</li><li><strong>Webinars &amp; Events</strong> &#8211; Monetizing your expertise through registration fees, premium content, or sponsor support.</li><li><strong>E-Learning &amp; Certifications</strong> &#8211; Offering continuing education that keeps professionals engaged and generates revenue.</li><li><strong>Merchandise</strong> &#8211; Creating branded items that members want to wear or use.</li><li><strong>Market Research</strong> &#8211; Packaging member insights into valuable, anonymized data for industry partners.</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When your team can match these tactics to real-world member needs, the magic begins.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Building Negotiation Confidence</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Negotiation can be intimidating, especially for staff who&#8217;ve never &#8220;sold&#8221; before.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Training should demystify the process, covering how to:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><li>Craft proposals that reflect member value</li><li>Lead with shared goals instead of price points</li><li>Build long-term sponsor relationships, not one-off deals</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>One client we worked with role-played sponsorship conversations during their team retreat. A shy program manager ended up closing a $10K partnership a month later, just by leading with mission and confidence.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Marketing That Moves People</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A well-designed product is only valuable if people know it exists. Marketing strategy is often the missing link in non-dues revenue training.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Equip your team to:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><li>Use storytelling to highlight impact, not just features</li><li>Create engaging emails that drive clicks, not confusion</li><li>Build social campaigns that feel personal, not pushy</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>And don&#8217;t underestimate internal marketing&#8212;helping your staff understand and champion new initiatives is just as important.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Embracing Technology for Scale</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You don&#8217;t need to be tech-savvy to leverage great tools. But your team does need to understand how to use:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><li>CRM systems to track sponsor engagement</li><li>LMS platforms to deliver paid courses</li><li>Analytics dashboards to measure campaign performance</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Training doesn&#8217;t have to be overwhelming. Start simple: one tool, one outcome, one win at a time.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Non-Dues Revenue Training Plan: 5 Steps to Get Started</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let&#8217;s turn theory into action.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Here&#8217;s a simple plan you can follow:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 1: Audit Your Current Revenue Landscape</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>What are your current income streams? Where are the gaps? Where are teams working in silos instead of collaborating? This snapshot will guide your training focus.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 2: Break Down Silos</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>What do you want your members to walk away with? Be specific. Whether it&#8217;s &#8220;understand how to implement a new HRIS system&#8221; or &#8220;complete DEI compliance training,&#8221; clarity here drives content and assessment design.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 3: Bring in Outside Expertise</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Sometimes, you need an external perspective. Hire a consultant or trainer who specializes in association revenue strategy. They&#8217;ll offer case studies, frameworks, and momentum that&#8217;s hard to build from the inside.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 4: Make It Real</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Theory is nice. Real-world examples are better. Include case studies from similar associations. Role-play tough conversations. Have your team pitch mock sponsorships. Get hands-on.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step 5: Keep Iterating</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Don&#8217;t treat training as a one-time event. Create a learning loop: test ideas, track results, gather feedback, adjust. This mindset turns training into transformation.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Myths vs. Truths About Non-Dues Revenue</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:table -->
<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Myth</th><th>Truth</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&#8220;Only big associations with large teams can do this.&#8221;</td><td>Even small associations can start simple, like offering a paid webinar or branded merchandise. It&#8217;s about strategy, not size.</td></tr><tr><td>&#8220;Non-dues revenue means selling out our mission.&#8221;</td><td>The right strategy reinforces your mission, not dilutes it. You&#8217;re adding value, not abandoning purpose.</td></tr><tr><td>&#8220;Our staff isn&#8217;t trained to sell or market.&#8221;</td><td>That&#8217;s the point of training. You&#8217;re not turning staff into salespeople&#8212;you&#8217;re helping them recognize value and share it authentically.</td></tr><tr><td>&#8220;It takes too long to see results.&#8221;</td><td>Many associations see quick wins within months, like sponsorship deals or pilot programs. Some revenue is better than none.</td></tr><tr><td>&#8220;We&#8217;ve tried before. It didn&#8217;t work.&#8221;</td><td>That doesn&#8217;t mean it can&#8217;t. With better alignment, support, and training, a second try can deliver real results.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>
<!-- /wp:table -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You&#8217;re not alone if some of those myths sounded familiar&#8212;many associations have been there. But here&#8217;s the good news: plenty have pushed past the doubt and achieved real results.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let&#8217;s look at a few associations that turned training into action and saw tangible success.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Real Wins: Associations That Profited from Revenue-Focused Training</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Still wondering if non-dues revenue training pays off? Here are a few real-world examples that show what&#8217;s possible when associations align their teams and invest in smart strategies:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">1. National Council of Architectural Registration Boards (NCARB)</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>At the 2024 RevUP Summit, NCARB was recognized for shifting toward a lean innovation mindset. They increased non-dues revenue by streamlining sponsorship models and adopting new packaging strategies for existing offerings, empowering their team to rethink revenue through collaboration.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Source: myPAR.org &#8211; RevUP Summit 2024</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">2. Mortgage Bankers Association (MBA)</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>MBA&#8217;s learning and development department bundled corporate training programs, offering scalable packages for companies seeking professional development solutions. This approach not only created new revenue but also supported their members&#8217; upskilling needs, proving that packaging + training = growth.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Source: Professionals for Association Revenue (PAR)</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">3. Society for Information Management (SIM)</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>SIM turned previously free offerings like their Women&#8217;s Leadership Forum and STEM initiative into sponsor-supported programs. They also added a virtual Cyber SIG conference and introduced native advertising opportunities&#8212;diversifying income while enhancing member value.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Source: Association Headquarters Case Study</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->
<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Kellen&#8217;s Healthcare Client Strategy</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A healthcare association managed by Kellen unlocked a $40,000 grant by aligning sponsors with a patient-impact initiative. By cross-pollinating ideas from other client strategies and emphasizing mission-aligned sponsorship, they created meaningful funding without compromising values.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Source: Kellen Company</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>These success stories are not unicorn data, but real examples of associations that moved beyond membership dues through focused training, strategic collaboration, and creative packaging. With the right approach, such results are entirely achievable.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What&#8217;s at Stake (and What&#8217;s Possible)</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If your association is struggling to balance budgets, retain members, or fuel new initiatives, you&#8217;re not alone. But here&#8217;s the good news:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>&#x1F3AF; You don&#8217;t have to reinvent the wheel.<br>&#x1F4A1; You just need to empower your team to see opportunities&#8212;and act on them.<br>&#x1F4C8; With the right training, non-dues revenue isn&#8217;t a gamble. It&#8217;s a game-changer.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ready to Get Started?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Your association already has the raw materials: smart people, passionate members, and a mission worth funding. With the right non-dues revenue training, you can build the bridge between where you are and where you want to be.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Whether you start with a lunch-and-learn, a half-day retreat, or a full strategic partnership, start now.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>And if you need support along the way, we&#8217;re here to help.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let&#8217;s build something sustainable, together.</p>
