('2610', '7', '2022-11-22 13:33:59', '2022-11-22 20:33:59', '<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":1} -->
<h1>How To Learn 2D Character Creation In Your Animated Online Course</h1>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Engagement is critical to successful eLearning. As course creators use more video animation in their courses, learner engagement is at an all-time high.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href=\"#title1\">The Benefits of 2D Characters</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href=\"#title2\">How to Create 2D Characters for<br />eLearning</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href=\"#title3\">The Bottom Line</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>As eLearning professionals, it’s our duty to up the ante. While eLearning video is a step in the right direction, storytelling and strong characters bring learning to life.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>2D Character Creation</strong>, in particular, is promising for eLearning. Takig the steps for how to learn 2D animation is simpler than 3D and it helps your learners form close, emotional bonds with the course material.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>But characters don’t appear out of thin air. The need to be created with intention, in ways that best serve the needs of your learners.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Benefits of 2D Character Creation</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It’s easy to dismiss characters in your eLearning videos. Character creation is an extra step, but it’s one that reaps big rewards for your course and your learners.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>2D animated characters have so many benefits for your learners.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Humans build emotional bonds with characters. Learning 2D animation helps you stand out and attract the interest of all learners, not just children. This motivates your learners to progress through the course and succeed.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>eLearning is convenient, but it can feel lonely. While self-directed learning is important, <a href=\"https://www.slideshare.net/brightcovecp/being-social-with-video\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learners need social connection</a> to engage with course content. 2D character creation & animation helps learners feel like they’re receiving one-on-one attention, which feels less isolating.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Want learners to use what they learn? Then it’s time to use 2D animated characters. Characters increase memory recall. Their strong personalities and storyline make learning more enjoyable, which, in turn, helps learners recall information.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Learning 2D animation helps you bridge the challenges and benefits of eLearning. You can easily provide students with human interaction while boosting retention, all thanks to character development.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->
<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to Create 2D Characters for eLearning</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you haven’t used 2D characters in your eLearning course, now is the time to start. Follow these five guidelines to create characters that further your learning goals.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Characters have to serve your audience’s learning objectives. Go into your 2D animation without preconceived notions for your characters. Instead, put yourself in your audience’s shoes. What type of character would your students like to see?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For example, corporate learners might prefer a realistic human character, while kids might enjoy whimsical characters. If you can, try polling your students for ideas. You don’t want to create a 2D character that doesn’t resonate with learner expectations.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Before you start mocking up your characters, plan how, exactly, they fit into your eLearning course. What will the character be doing? Will they stand relatively still, or zoom around the screen? Do they need particular clothing or props based on your script?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The form of your character has to allow a certain amount of function. It’s best to go with a simple design for your 2D character creation. This will make it easier for learners to understand the character. It also makes it easier for you to do more dynamic poses with simple characters. Take these considerations into account before designing your character. You don’t want to build a character whose arms don’t function correctly for your script.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>After you build a functional wireframe of your character, it’s time to apply styling. The style or aesthetic of your character is what makes them visually appealing to learners. The goal is to have a trademark style that learners immediately recognize as yours. Remember that the aesthetic does need to match the tone and content of your script. You don’t want a loony, over-the-top character if your course material is more serious.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Personality is key to the success of your 2D animation. For animated characters, much of their personality ties in to their physical appearance. For example, Eeyore is gray and has sunken eyes. This is done on purpose to communicate his personality.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Exaggerate certain features depending on the character’s personality. If you don’t know where to start, begin by listing adjectives or verbs that describe your character. Add features that correlate to those adjectives and verbs. This is a key component for how to learn 2D animation.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Time to go to the drawing board.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Remember that 2D character creation is a process. You aren’t going to create a finalized character in your first sketch. Test different expressions and poses for the character. Focus on the character’s emotion, and how that serves your eLearning content.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>2D animated characters are a clever way to boost learner retention and engagement. Invest in learning quality 2D animation for your eLearning video to see happier learners and more repeat students.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Read More:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

