('2626', '7', '2022-11-15 09:46:46', '2022-11-15 16:46:46', '<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":1} -->
<h1>Create a Better Course by Interviewing a Subject Matter Expert (SME)</h1>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>How do you create an awesome eLearning and<br />microlearning video course? You’re an experienced<br />eLearning professional.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href=\"#title1\">The Benefits of Video Training</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href=\"#title2\">The Bottom Line</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>But while you’re well-versed on instructional design, you still need an outside, expert perspective on your course content. A subject matter expert, or SME, is a critical part of creating an effective eLearning and microlearning course for your organization.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The SME could be someone within your organization who’s well-versed in a particular software. It could also be an expert in the field, like an author or researcher. No matter where you find them, SMEs add a level of credibility, depth, and relevance to your course. They’re also critical to keep your course on track during the planning phase.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Every eLearning course needs an SME. But SMEs are busy, and that means you need to make the most of your time with them. Treat SMEs as the valuable resource they are without monopolizing their time. Follow these 3 tips to get the most out of interviewing an SME for your eLearning course.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Do your homework before you approach an SME. As an instructional designer, you should already have an idea of your learning objectives for the course. Once you know the learning objectives, you can work with your SME to advise on the best behaviors, activities, and information to focus on in your course.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You need to prepare for working with your SME before reaching out to them. This will give the SME an idea of how they can best help you, as well as keep your discussions with them relevant and useful.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Before you ask the SME for a meeting, brainstorm an in-depth questionnaire first. It’s important to distill your questions down to the most important information. Anything superfluous is a waste of your SME’s time and won’t add any value to your course Remember, this questionnaire should be just long enough to glean good information from the SME. Eliminate any questions that won’t add value to your eLearning video course.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When writing your questionnaire:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Consider asking questions like:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Once you’ve prepared the learning objectives and a questionnaire, write an agenda for the discussion. This will honor the SME’s limited amount of time and help you stay on-task during the meeting.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>The agenda should show:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Agenda in hand, now’s the time to ask your SME for a meeting. This is easy if the SME is within your organization, but if they’re not, you’ll need to email them to request a meeting. The agenda can help you secure the meeting because it gives SMEs a great idea of what you plan to discuss. If they agree, the agenda preps the SME so they can give you better information.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Before the meeting, make sure you brush up on industry jargon. Your expertise is in learning design, but if you’re designing a course for medical staff, you might not be familiar with those professional terms. Ensure you know just enough to facilitate a good conversation with the SME.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For the meeting itself, invest in a screen recording software like Screencast-O-Matic. Always ask permission to record the meeting. This is critical because you’ll want to refer to their expertise while designing the course itself; written notes from the interview will only get you so far.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>An SME helps you get into the mind of your learner. Designing a course is an art unto itself, but you can improve the substance of your course with an SME’s insight. But if you want to make the most of an SME’s expertise, you need to structure the interview carefully.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Get better results for your organization. Design a course that changes learner behavior for the better with an SME’s real-world insights. You’ll boost employee satisfaction and your organization’s success in the process.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>But what if you want to include an SME in the course itself? We’ve got you covered. Ninja Tropic specializes in SME-driven eLearning and microlearning courses.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Get in touch now for your FREE brainstorming session with our team of video pros.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

